{{nihongo|'''Yanbaru'''|山原}}, officially the '''State of Yanbaru''', is a [[w:Sovereign state|sovereign island nation]] located in the northern region of the Sanese Archipelago on the planet Adonia. It shares a maritime border with the Imperial Kingdom of [[Okaiken]] to the south and sits at a critical [[w:Choke point|maritime chokepoint]] in the [[Shendan Ocean]]. Yanbaru is renowned for its culturally distinct indigenous heritage, having maintained deep historical ties as a crucial [[w:Vassal state|vassal state]] and trade intermediary for the [[Kingdom of Zong]] during the Fosian [[w:Colonialism|colonial era]].

== Etymology ==
The name {{nihongo|Yanbaru|山原}} translates literally to mountain fields or mountainous wilderness, derived from the characters for mountain (山, yama) and field or plain (原, haru or baru). According to early Sanese chronicles and indigenous oral traditions, the name was first coined by early maritime settlers and traders arriving from the central islands of Okaiken and the Fosian mainland.

As these early navigators approached the northern archipelago, they were struck by the imposing, densely forested mountains that rose sharply from the coastal plains of Nakashima. To distinguish this rugged, untamed geography from the flatter agricultural centers of the south, they referred to the entire northern island chain as the Yanbaru region. Over centuries of maritime consolidation and eventual political unification under the kings of Yomitan, this geographic descriptor was formally adopted as the official name of the sovereign state.

== History ==

=== Prehistory ===
{{main|Prehistoric Yanbaru}}
The first human settlement of the Yanbaru archipelago dates to approximately 50,000 BP, when maritime voyagers from the [[Jade Peninsula]] crossed the straits in small watercraft to reach the islands. Unlike the southern regions of Fosia, the Yanbaru archipelago was never connected to the mainland by land bridges, requiring early seafaring capability for colonization. A second wave of migrants arrived from the central Sanese islands during the Neolithic period (~15,000–3,000 BCE), intermixing with the existing Paleolithic population to form the genetic and cultural foundation of the indigenous Yanbaru people.

The '''Nakashima Culture''' ({{circa|15,000|300|BCE}}) emerged from this admixture, distinguished by its specialized exploitation of coral reef ecosystems. The culture developed unique maritime adaptations including complex techniques for harvesting reef fish, crustaceans, and mollusks; collection of tortoiseshell, red coral, and pearls; and the production of shell tools and outrigger canoes suited to navigation through coral reefs and mangrove channels. The prehistoric period ended around 150 BCE, when the [[Kingdom of Donghai]] first documented the northern island tribes as tribute-paying polities, marking the beginning of the historical era.

=== The Coral Chiefdoms and the Pirate Sanctuary ===
[[File:Yanbaru_Coral_Reefs.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Traditional Yanbarun outrigger canoes navigating the warm, shallow coral reefs during the early Chiefdom era.]]
Because the Sanese archipelago is situated in the southern hemisphere, navigating north from the temperate central plains of [[Okaiken]] means entering increasingly warm, equatorial latitudes. For centuries, the northern archipelago of Yanbaru operated as a fiercely independent, tropical maritime frontier. Populated by indigenous seafaring tribes who navigated the complex [[w:Coral reef|coral reefs]] and dense mangroves, early Yanbaru was a fragmented collection of coastal chiefdoms. They thrived by harvesting rare equatorial goods—such as tortoiseshell, red coral, pearls, and tropical hardwoods—and trading them with early Fosian empires.

Yanbaru's geopolitical landscape shifted dramatically during the 14th century. As the unified Miyagi Empire to the south fractured during the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]], the resulting Kitayama period in Okaiken led to a severe collapse in central naval policing. Defeated samurai, exiled lords, and the infamous Sea Wolves (powerful, heavily armed pirate fleets) fled north across the straits, seeking refuge in the warm, lawless islands of Yanbaru. The archipelago absorbed these rogue elements, who used the hidden coves to build secret fleets and launch devastating raids southward. The sudden influx of Okaiken military tactics, foreign firearms, and pirate wealth caused the scattered indigenous chiefdoms to consolidate into three rival tropical principalities, constantly warring for control of the lucrative equatorial trade winds.

=== Classical period and Zong vassalage ===
[[File:Zong_Treasure_Fleet_Yanbaru.png|thumb|left|250px|A Zong treasure junk anchored in the fortified harbor of Yomitan, facilitating the flow of Shendan Ocean wealth.]]
Yanbaru eventually unified under a single monarch, ending the era of the three principalities. As an independent maritime kingdom, Yanbaru served as a vital cultural crossroads between the Zong Empire and the coastal states of [[Fosia]]. Following the [[Jade Sea War]] in the 14th century, the [[Kingdom of Zong]] was cut off from western overland and Mesogean [[w:Trade route|trade routes]]. Shut out from these markets, the Zong redirected their maritime energies eastward across the [[Shendan Ocean]] in the 1430s.

Drawing on the [[w:Shipbuilding|shipbuilding capacity]] and navigational knowledge developed during the war, Zong utilized Yanbaru as its primary springboard into the unknown. Initially operating as a traditional Fosian [[w:Trading diaspora|trading diaspora]] operating under local license, Yanbaru was rapidly integrated as a formal, highly prioritized vassal state of the Zong crown. As Zong established permanent outposts and the major [[w:Colony|colony]] of [[Tamsui]] on the western coast of [[Lurandia]], the immense wealth of this trans-oceanic empire—comprising silks, ceramics, spices, and lacquerware—flowed directly through Yanbaru's tropical ports. This era transformed the indigenous kingdom into a highly prosperous, fortified maritime hub, shielding it from the Sengoku periods that simultaneously ravaged the central Sanese states to the south.

=== The Great Disappointment and the Treaty of Yomitan ===
[[File:Ernesto_Lacuna_Yomitan.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Volisanian explorer Ernesto Lacuña negotiating the Treaty of Yomitan with Yanbarun officials in 1613.]]
In 1564, the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian]] Expedició del Ponent, commanded by Berenguer de Montcada, crossed the [[New World]] isthmus and encountered the Zong colony of Tamsui. This event, known in [[Illypnia]] as the [[Great Disappointment]], shattered the illusion of an uncontested western frontier and introduced a new dimension of global colonial warfare. However, it also opened highly lucrative new avenues of trans-oceanic trade.

During this period of global upheaval, Yanbaru was ruled by {{nihongo|King Shō Ryū|尚龍}}. Recognizing the existential threat posed by the encroaching Illypnian powers, King Shō Ryū fortified the coastal capital of Yomitan and strictly reaffirmed Yanbaru's loyalty to the Zong Emperor. He authorized the massive expansion of Zong [[w:Naval base|naval garrisons]] on the islands, ensuring that Yanbaru remained an impenetrable fortress safeguarding the Shendan trade routes even as colonial skirmishes erupted across the ocean.

As a shorter trade circuit developed bridging Illypnian goods across the Aethiopean Ocean with Fosian goods across the Shendan Ocean, Yanbaru became the indispensable intermediary hub. By the early 17th century, the island's strategic ports were crawling with displaced Sanese [[w:Piracy|pirates]] and rogue merchants exploiting the localized skirmishes between Illypnian and Fosian colonial interests. Between 1610 and 1613, the Volisanian explorer [[Ernesto Lacuña]] launched a series of daring naval expeditions northward from the colonial capital of Cinco Muros in [[Dagit]]. Tasked with mapping the northern boundaries of the Fosian sphere and securing a buffer zone for Volisanian shipping, Lacuña commanded a small but technologically advanced fleet.

After navigating treacherous coral reefs and surviving the fringes of the devastating Eastern Typhoons, Lacuña's expedition finally arrived at Yomitan. He found the harbor bristling with defensive [[w:Star fort|star-fortifications]] commissioned by King Shō Ryū decades prior, supported by a formidable presence of Zong war [[w:Junk (ship)|junks]] and thousands of seasoned Sanese [[w:Mercenary|mercenaries]]. Rather than engaging in a suicidal naval bombardment against a heavily fortified Zong vassal, Lacuña utilized the immense wealth generated by the Dagit colonies to effectively buy out the local mercenary contracts. This successful [[w:Diplomacy|diplomatic maneuvering]] culminated in the Treaty of Yomitan, a localized agreement with the Yanbaru authorities. The treaty permitted Volisanian merchant vessels to utilize the ports for resupply in exchange for military protection against aggressive Zong retaliation. This delicate balancing act allowed Yanbaru to maintain its lucrative autonomy, acting as a neutral, tropical buffer zone between the competing Illypnian and Fosian colonial spheres.

=== The 1625 Crisis and diplomatic neutralization ===
[[File:Honda_Diplomatic_Mission_Yanbaru.png|thumb|left|250px|Hotta Muneshige's high-level condolence embassy arriving in Yomitan, successfully averting a Yanbarun invasion during the Kinzō crisis.]]
In the summer of 1625, the central Sanese state of Mitoyagi was plunged into a severe succession crisis following the assassination of Retired [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] and the death of Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]]. Sensing a collapse of the Honda Shogunate, Yanbaru—alongside the freezing southern nation of [[Nakamizu]]—deployed warships to loiter near the Sea of Mitoyagi, probing for territorial concessions and floating disingenuous offers of mediation. The strategic ports of Yanbaru teemed with displaced Sanese mercenaries eager to exploit the chaos.

However, the new shogun, [[Honda Shigetoki]], and his regent [[Hotta Muneshige]] executed a masterclass in crisis management that swiftly neutralized the Yanbarun threat. Within ten days of the crisis, Muneshige dispatched a high-level condolence embassy to the court in Yomitan, carrying a personal letter from Shigetoki that invited Yanbarun representatives to witness a grand memorial in Okami. As an economic incentive, the shogunate temporarily opened the lucrative Mitoyagi rice futures market to Yanbarun merchants, provided they attended the state funeral in person.

Concurrently, the Honda fleet conducted massive, highly visible naval unity exercises in the straits, signaling that the military government remained fully capable of repelling any mercenary incursions. To permanently secure the northern maritime border, Shigetoki quietly negotiated a lesser alliance marriage with the Yanbarun royal family. By framing the 1625 crisis as the definitive end of the Sengoku era, Muneshige made it politically toxic for Yanbaru to intervene. Within months, Yanbaru transformed from an opportunistic predator into an invested stakeholder, laying the groundwork for unprecedented economic cooperation.

=== The Maritime Accords and the Lurandian diaspora ===
[[File:Shisa_Guardian_Statue.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A traditional Yanbarun ''Shisa'' statue. These guardian beasts were heavily exported to Okaiken and Sanese enclaves in Lurandia following the Maritime Accords.]]
In the 1630s, following the diplomatic stabilization of the 1625 crisis, Yanbaru entered into further negotiations with Shogun Honda Shigetoki. Abandoning the strict [[w:Isolationism|isolationist policies]] of his predecessors, Shigetoki established the {{nihongo|Maritime Accords of Okami|岡見海事協定}} with the Yanbarun court in Yomitan. This landmark agreement allowed central Sanese merchants to safely bypass heavy Zong [[w:Tariff|tariffs]] by chartering Yanbarun vessels. As Okaiken later implemented its own strict maritime prohibitions under the fourth shogun, [[Honda Shigemasa]], Yanbaru became the singular, vital loophole that integrated Okaiken into the global trade network without requiring a vulnerable deep-water navy.

Through these stabilized trade routes, Yanbaru became the primary catalyst for the early Sanese [[w:Diaspora|diaspora]]. Utilizing its deep connections with the Zong Empire and the southern state of Nakamizu, Yanbaru facilitated the establishment of distinct expatriate communities within the Zong colonies of the New World, particularly in Tamsui on the continent of Lurandia. Many displaced Mitoyagi [[w:Samurai|samurai]], left landless after their own internal conflicts and fleeing the oppressive purges of the Honda Shogunate, were permitted to emigrate through Yanbaru. They traveled across the Shendan Ocean to serve as mercenaries, artisans, and merchants in these distant Lurandian settlements.

This era of unprecedented trade also triggered a massive cultural export. Exposed to Yanbarun maritime aesthetics through the bustling tropical ports, Shogun Shigetoki became fascinated by {{nihongo|Shisa|シーサー}}, the traditional guardian beast statues used in the northern archipelago. He commissioned hundreds of these stone guardians for his own capital, leading to the widespread adoption of Shisa culture throughout Okaiken. Furthermore, the statues were heavily exported to the newly established Sanese enclaves in Lurandia, where the diaspora used them to symbolically protect their colonial settlements from the unknown perils of the New World.

=== Modern era ===
[[File:Modern_Yomitan_Harbor.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The bustling, modernized port of Yomitan in the 20th century, reflecting Yanbaru's transition into a constitutional monarchy.]]
Having successfully navigated the colonial era by playing rival empires against one another and serving as the indispensable middleman for Okaiken's closed economy, Yanbaru modernized its economy over the 19th and 20th centuries while strictly preserving its unique indigenous identity. Unlike the central state of Okaiken, which underwent rapid imperial expansion and militarization, Yanbaru maintained a policy of peaceful maritime commerce and cultural preservation, eventually transitioning into a modern constitutional monarchy renowned for its tropical resorts and immense historical wealth.

== Geography ==
Yanbaru is the northernmost nation within the Sanese Archipelago, sharing the broader [[w:Archipelago|island chain]] with Okaiken and Nakamizu. The geography is characterized by rugged, [[w:Topography|mountainous interiors]] and expansive [[w:Coastal plain|coastal plains]]. Because the eastern coastlines are frequently battered by massive [[w:Cyclone|cyclonic systems]] known as the Eastern Typhoons, the islands feature highly distinct architecture. Buildings are traditionally defined by heavy stone walls and red-tiled roofs adorned with protective guardian beast statues known as {{nihongo|Shisa|シーサー}}, designed to withstand severe meteorological disruptions.

The country is primarily divided into three major landmasses. The central and largest island is Nakashima, which holds the vast majority of the population, agricultural plains, and the political center of Yomitan. To the northwest lies the smaller, deeply forested island of Irikashima, known for its sheer coastal cliffs and secluded deep-water inlets. To the southeast is Agarishima, a flatter, coral-ringed island that serves as the nation's primary barrier against the cyclonic storms brewing in the Shendan Ocean.

== Administrative divisions ==
Yanbaru is a [[w:Unitary state|unitary state]] divided into 10 primary [[w:Administrative division|administrative districts]]. In a preservation of indigenous heritage, the government officially utilizes the historical indigenous classification of [[w:Magiri|Magiri]] for these first-level divisions. Each Magiri is governed by an elected prefectural assembly and is named after its capital and largest city. The municipalities within them have fairly broad powers; they control public education, local maritime laws, and are authorized to levy taxes.

